---
const roomCalculatorUrl = '/en/find-my-tv-size';
const distanceCalculatorUrl = '/en/viewing-distance';
const comparisonUrl = '/en/compare';
---

<section class="border-t border-[var(--line)] bg-[var(--surface)] py-20 sm:py-28" aria-labelledby="tv-size-calculator-guide">
	<div class="site-shell">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
			<p class="eyebrow">TV buying guide</p>
			<h2 id="tv-size-calculator-guide" class="mt-4 text-balance text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-[-0.045em] text-[var(--text)] sm:text-5xl">
				How to use the TV size calculator
			</h2>
			<p class="mt-6 text-pretty text-lg leading-8 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
				Choosing a television by diagonal measurement alone can be misleading. Our free <strong class="font-medium text-[var(--text)]">TV size calculator</strong> connects screen dimensions with the distance from your couch, so you can see whether a TV will feel cinematic, balanced, or too small before you buy it. Enter your setup, compare common screen sizes, and use the visual preview to make a practical decision for your actual room.
			</p>

			<div class="mt-12 grid gap-10 border-t border-[var(--line)] pt-12 md:grid-cols-2 md:gap-x-14 md:gap-y-12">
				<div>
					<h3 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-[var(--text)]">Start with your seating position</h3>
					<p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
						Choosing a screen for a room starts with viewing distance: the straight-line measurement from your eyes in the usual seating position to the screen. Our <a class="font-medium text-accent underline decoration-accent/35 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-accent" href={roomCalculatorUrl}>room-based size calculator</a> converts that distance into recommended diagonals for three viewing styles. Immersive suits films and games, balanced works well for mixed everyday use, and relaxed provides a smaller image that may feel comfortable for casual viewing.
					</p>
					<p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
						Measure from the seating position, not from wall to wall. Then account for a TV stand, wall mount, reclined seating, and any walkway between the couch and screen. This makes the result more useful than a generic chart. The room preview helps you judge the recommendation in context.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div>
					<h3 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-[var(--text)]">Viewing distance and field of view</h3>
					<p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
						Our <a class="font-medium text-accent underline decoration-accent/35 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-accent" href={distanceCalculatorUrl}>viewing-distance calculator</a> uses field of view to describe how much of your horizontal vision the picture occupies. A larger screen or a closer seat creates a wider, more immersive view. A smaller screen or greater distance creates a narrower, more relaxed view. The simulator provides 40°, 30°, and 25° reference points so you can compare the experience instead of relying on one rigid answer.
					</p>
					<p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
						Use the distance control to test nearby positions, especially when several people sit at different depths. If your sofa cannot move, adjust the screen diagonal. If the TV size is fixed, adjust the seat. You can try both changes in a new room or an existing entertainment area.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div>
					<h3 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-[var(--text)]">What changes with 4K screens?</h3>
					<p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
						A 4K screen can show finer detail at close range when the source provides it. The calculator uses physical screen size and seating distance; it does not change its recommendations based on resolution. Resolution is only one part of the choice, however. Content quality, eyesight, room lighting, subtitles, gaming habits, and personal comfort all matter. Treat the calculated size as a strong starting point, then use the visual tools to compare the options immediately above and below it.
					</p>
					<p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
						Remember that advertised TV size is the diagonal of the display panel, not its width. For a standard 16:9 screen, width and height grow together, so screen area rises much faster than the diagonal number suggests. Our <a class="font-medium text-accent underline decoration-accent/35 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-accent" href={comparisonUrl}>true-to-scale TV comparison</a> shows that difference clearly and lists the actual width, height, and screen-area change between two sizes.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div>
					<h3 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-[var(--text)]">Find your ideal TV size with confidence</h3>
					<p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
						For the most accurate result, measure your normal eye position in feet, choose the viewing style that matches your habits, and check the suggested reasonable range. The calculation may return a number between common retail sizes; when that happens, compare the closest available models. Pick the larger option for stronger immersion or the smaller one for a gentler view and easier placement.
					</p>
					<p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
						Finally, confirm the television’s listed cabinet dimensions before ordering. Bezels, feet, stands, soundbars, and wall clearances are not included in the 16:9 screen calculation. By combining room measurements, viewing distance, field of view, and true screen geometry, RealTVSize gives you a clearer answer than diagonal size alone—and helps you choose a screen that feels right after it enters the room.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>
